The invariant is that entry requests and current definitions have
separate identities even after an entry merge is completed. If $m$
really is redundant, replacement must redirect references to $m$,
including any table entry that still names it, not all references
indexed by $x$. Delay simplification of a merge until its operands
are filled; testing it after seeing only the first predecessor could
lose the backedge. Excluding recursive queries, sealing costs one
operand attachment per pending merge and incoming edge. Using edge
identities rather than source names also preserves separate operand
positions for multiple edges emitted by one predecessor.

\section{Check the Builder at the Boundary}

For each reachable edge into a sealed block, the operand of an entry merge must equal the value the source binding has at that predecessor's exit. Ordinary uses must refer to dominating definitions; an incoming operand must be available on its edge, not necessarily throughout the destination block. These checks apply to finished IR, not to the builder's private incomplete state. Test a zero-trip loop and a multi-iteration loop, then add a header that both reads and overwrites a binding before sealing. For a second oracle, run the frontier-based builder on the same complete CFG and compare observable execution, not the names or exact number of merges. The dossier's Chapter~14, \emph{Interpreter as a Compiler}, suggests a natural setting for such a reference evaluator; Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-210} explains why structural verification alone is not that oracle.
